1. d4 { Notes by Geza Maroczy } 1... Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. e3 b6 4. Nbd2 Bb7 5. Be2 { Alekhine thinks that Bd3 is better. } 5... Be7 6. O-O O-O 7. b3 c5 8. Bb2 Nc6 9. Ne5 Qc7 10. f4 { This costs a pawn;
Watson played against Rubinstein Bf3. } 10... Nd5 11. Rf3 cxd4 12. exd4 f6 13. Nd3 Ncb4 14. Nxb4 Bxb4 { Much better than Nxb4
whereby White could obtain a strong attack for instance 15 Rh3
Qxf4 16 a3 Nd5 17 Bd3 g6 18 c4 Ne3 19 Qe2 Nf5 20 Rf1, etc. } 15. c4 Nxf4 16. Rf2 e5 { ! } 17. a3 Bd6 18. Bf1 e4 { The passed
pawn decides the game. There is no further hope for White. } 19. g3 e3 20. gxf4 exf2+ 21. Kxf2 Bxf4 22. Nf3 Bxh2 23. d5 Qg3+ 24. Ke2 Rae8+ 25. Kd2 Qf2+ 26. Kc1 Bf4+ 27. Kb1 Re3 0-1

