1. e4 { Notes by Dr. Savielly Tartakower. } 1... e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Nxe4 6. d4 b5 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5 Be6 9. c3 Be7 10. Nbd2 Nc5 11. Bc2 Bg4 12. h3 { The most reasonable course here
is 12.Re1, guarding the e-pawn. } 12... Bh5 13. Qe1 { ?! Here again
13.Re1 ensured a very good game for White. } 13... Ne6 14. Nh2 { ?! } 14... Bg6 { ! } 15. Bxg6 fxg6 { ! Far seeing strategy! Black recognizes
that the f-file and not the e-file will be needed as a base
for action. } 16. Nb3 { Or 16.f4 d4!. } 16... g5 { ! } 17. Be3 O-O 18. Nf3 Qd7 19. Qd2 { White pays insufficient attention to the scope of
his opponent's threats. A better course is 19.Nfd4 (19...Nxe5
20.Bxg5) seeking to establish equality. } 19... Rxf3 { ! } 20. gxf3 Nxe5 21. Qe2 Rf8 22. Nd2 Ng6 23. Rfe1 Bd6 24. f4 Nexf4 25. Qf1 Nxh3+ 26. Kh1 g4 27. Qe2 Qf5 0-1

